                <pb facs="0845" n="749" sameAs="frus:frus1874/pg749"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in 1 In No.
                    143.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Mejia to Mr. Lafragua.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of War, Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1874-04-25"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >April</hi> 25, 1874</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#italic">Citizen Minister of Foreign
                        Affairs:</hi></p>
                <p>In a telegram of yesterday, from Matamoras, the citizen colonel, José L. Cristo,
                    makes to me the following statement:</p>
                <p>“Referring to your two telegrams of the 19th and 20th received to-day, Alexander
                    Hamilton is absent; it has been publicly said that he took passage for Mexico,
                    via Monterey, in the diligence of the 12th of February, under the name of Thomas
                    M. Morgan. The American consulate does not know where he is; should he be
                    discovered, I will proceed to obey the order of your ministry. The extradition
                    was demanded of the court of instance, which sent me a requisition for his
                    arrest. I replied that the troops were at its service, and no further
                    communication was made me. By mail I send the printed correspondence.”</p>
                <p>I transcribe the above as the result of your communication on the subject.</p>
                <p>Independence and liberty!</p>
                <closer><signed>MEJIA.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
